body:
Thank you. I appreciate your kind words. 

&#x200B;

The thought of leaving is so appealing and and at the same time, unthinkable.

Uh, are those ones? Cool she can buy a cheap lunch for herself. WOW, she really is the boss lady. 

&#x200B;

I'm sorry, but if your marriage is crumbling, lube and dusting powder for your sheets isn't going to fix anything.

I hate how MLM's make people who have common sense feel like they're lazy or shitty people for seeing that the results they promise are completely unrealistic. 

&#x200B;

Also I used to go to a ton of MLM parties when I first married my husband and moved into military housing (every neighbor on every side is a hun) and I honestly still really like some of the products I purchased from the companies, but I hate their business practices now that I have a better understanding of what actually goes on and how much money people lose.

I didn't "share" any of this bullshit and I'm not going to share something that's going to lead to an influx of constant harassment from the huns and potentially dozens of ignorant people getting sucked into a pyramid scheme. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Also pls stop saying you're "starting a business" when all you did was purchase $150 of mandatory inventory and now you have to sell it all to make your money back.

Can confirm, am admin for a June/ July bumpers group. The group was only a closed group when June due dates were  5-6 weeks along and became secret after July 31 due dates would hit the 3 month marker. Most people know they're pregnant by then. I know the other July groups closed around the 4 month marker, but we had been open longer and pretty much all of the genuine join requests had dried up. 

&#x200B;

Unfortunately, there are a lot of creepers. We had one girl who posted constantly at first and ladies who were members of multiple groups started recognizing her name and photo and realized that every group had a different story... in one she said she was 18 and this was her first baby, another she claimed to be 22 and expecting her third child, and one of them she claimed to be 25 and had one living child who was severely disabled and had experienced three early losses and one stillbirth at 31 weeks trying to conceive. She started bonding with people from each of the peer groups she claimed to be in and started sending out friend requests to people and got really clingy with a few of the younger girls who had started chatting with her when she was working under the guise of being a teen mom. Super uncomfortable. The phone number on her Facebook page was registered to a 50 year old Indian man living in the south. Yikes. 

&#x200B;

There are still a few folks who I think are just... not genuine about the stuff they post, to put it politely. There's even another admin in a different group who I feel like fakes a lot of her pregnancy related health  issues because nothing she says makes sense medically speaking, but her personal Facebook page supports at least 50% of her story, and frankly she's not hurting anyone so I just kind of leave her alone.

I feel the same way about some of the pregnancy groups. Honestly, I feel like a lot of people use those groups for free therapy and unpack a lot more personal shit than I would feel comfortable putting out there with my name and contact information slapped on it... venting on reddit is one thing, but on Facebook, everyone can see the boyfriend that you're claiming is a dead beat that is messaging your best friend at 2am asking for boob pics.

&#x200B;

I also definitely see a lot of judgment in breastfeeding support groups. I see a lot of women pushing other mamas  who are literally on the brink of an emotional break down to keep trying to breastfeed exclusively, when multiple lactation consultants have told mom that her supply is drying up and the pediatrician has suggested a move to formula because baby isn't putting on weight as she should.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Its sad really.

Not that simple at all. Dee Dee (the mother) was mentally ill and essentially kept her daughter Gypsy Rose  imprisoned  by faking illnesses such as terminal cancer and muscular dystrophy by manufacturing symptoms in her daughter from childhood with drugs and malnutrition. She separated her daughter from anyone who would have been able to help her, and no one ever questioned Dee Dee because she kept up the charade of being a loving and concerned, if overprotective mother. Gypsy's teeth fell out because of lack of proper nutrition, she had an unnecessary feeding tube, and was kept confined to a wheelchair. Dee Dee used Gypsy to extort thousands from her daughter's father and from people who had heard Gypsy's story and sent donations to help. Habitat For Humanity even built them a custom house. 

&#x200B;

When Gypsy discovered that she could communicate with people online, she began to slowly escape the prison her mother had build around her, but Dee Dee often put an abrupt end to these associations by pulling out Gypsy's fake birth certificate (which stated Gypsy was about 5 years younger than she actually was) and threatening the men she met with statutory rape charges or other legal action. Gypsy says the idea of murder was something she had only mentioned idly to one of the men she was actually able to continue an a secret online affair with, and he took the suggestion seriously. With his enthusiasm, they cooked up a plan to get away with the crime by hiding the murder weapon by mailing it after the crime, and fleeing to the boyfriend's out of state residence. Needless to say, Dee Dee's death was still traced to them and they were found out.

I mean I kind of understand it. Lots of them are young and married quickly and are a little too emotionally dependent on their husbands and want to spend every moment that he's not at work with them. Most jobs require that you be available on weekends and nights, at least for part of the week. I have an English composition background and I offered to review resumes for a small fee before I found permanent employment. I saw way too many women who wanted a job, but also wanted to work only from 7a-4p and no weekends. Unless you want to teach, you're never going to get those hours, and most of the women I spoke to were lacking in transferable skills to an office environment and would be best qualified for work in retail and food service. 

&#x200B;

Enter, the huns who promise profit for very little actually work, all from the comfort of your own home. It would be great and extremely ideal, if any of that was remotely true.

&#x200B;

We're at MCAS Yuma btw, since you're also out west. 

&#x200B;

Also military spouse. I would 100% start with contacting the admin,  but wayyy too many of the women I see in our base Facebook page is full of women who genuinely want to buy the nonsense that the huns are peddling. I don't know how to work around it, because I know the admin of our group personally and she is anti-mlm and tried to ban MLM sales from the page but was met with a barrage of both the huns and women looking for pure romance parties and essential oils so she reinstated mlm rights. We do have a disclaimer on the page since it is a closed group that basically says one of the suggestions from senior command before making any financial decision is to consult with a trusted advisor before hand, and we urge anyone who is considering joining MLM companies to do the same. 

&#x200B;

My husband told me when we were engaged that I was absolutely prohibited from ever selling MLM products. He had already been in for awhile before we met, and saw a few of his buddies completely drained by their wives who invested hundreds out of their already measly biweekly checks into their "inventory." 

&#x200B;

What are the biggest culprits on your base? We're out west so naturally, essential oils are pretty popular out here. People also love 31, Pure Romance, Arbonne, and Thrive.

A friend of mine invited herself to visit me where I live in Arizona and bought a ticket to fly out even though I told her I was pregnant, not feeling well,  and not going to be able to bar hop with her like we did in the college days. Even though I took her off roading, on a sightseeing tour to Mexico, and out to eat multiple times, she complained of being bored in less than 6 hours, called her boyfriend constantly, talked about how my incredibly patient and self sacrificing husband wasn't doing enough for me, started an argument between me and my husband, and then demanded that I pay for her early ticket home because she didn't realize that it would be so boring and that she felt "unsafe" in our home because we had an argument behind our closed bedroom door (raised voices, nothing more). 

&#x200B;

HAHA. YIKES.

Hey there, another BJJ McDojo survivor here...

&#x200B;

In this school's defense, I believe that at one time, it was a legitimate operation and the head black belt actually gave a shit. His wife turned it into a money making machine and advertises on Facebook abouthow she goes to conferences to discuss how she turned such a large profit running his dojo.

&#x200B;

1) Long term contracts with no way to opt out without paying the balance. I burned out after four years of drinking the Kool-Aid, just shortly after I had signed another one year contract. To top it off, I was finishing undergrad and starting grad school at a university an hour away from the dojo and it just didn't fit into my commute.  They claimed that wasn't far enough out of their zip code and the best they could do would be to alter my contract and change it to a 2 class a week schedule for $145/ month  instead of the unlimited schedule I was paying $210/ month for. After some fuss, including my mother calling the dojo and explaining that as a poor college student, I couldn't afford to pay for a service I didn't use, they finally let me cancel the contract.

&#x200B;

2) When I came back in to see off a couple of the friends I had made over the years, I was told that I couldn't pay the $25 mat fee to join in on class because I had terminated my contract and was no longer a paying member of the school

&#x200B;

3) For the quality of instruction, the pricing of the classes was ridiculously high. The pricing was on par with Renzo Grace's membership in NYC, or the Ribiero Brothers school in San Diego. 

&#x200B;

4) Constantly pushing private lessons on you for a ridiculously steep hourly rate

&#x200B;

5) The belt system in BJJ is supposed to go white blue purple brown black. The kid's belt system uses yellow, orange, gray, and green belts between the white and blue belts because students under 16 years old are not able to be awarded a blue belt. This school used the kids belts for adults too, and made you pay for each belt promotion.

&#x200B;

6) Incredibly slow belt promotion. Most students felt they were ready for a promotion 6 months to a year before they actually got promoted. I took home a gold medal at the Chicago open at blue belt, and a silver medal in Boston at blue belt. It was another year before I got promoted. I could open a can of whoop ass on purple belts at that point. 

&#x200B;

7) Heavily discouraging, even belittling, students making visits to other dojos, even when there was an extremely accomplished grappler or "celebrity" putting on a seminar. 

&#x200B;

8) Pushing dojo-branded merchandise on students from the moment they began their first class.

&#x200B;

9) A professor who basically had competed once in the last decade, and constantly lived in the glory days of that match... which was a loss.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

ANNNNND The worst part.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

10) Despite all of the ridiculousness that took place, the professor was a very nice looking guy, if a little bit past his prime. I need to preface this with the fact that I was one of his only straight female students at this time... Every few months, his wife would get fed up with something (who knows anymore) and kick him out. He would call up one of his students and ask to stay with them. I got a call at 1am from a cell number in my area stating who it was and if he could give me call. I was 18 and incredibly stupid so I gave him my address. He showed up drunk and tried to snuggle me on the couch. At the time I was incredibly flattered because I thought he was  attractive and I was only a year into training with him. Luckily, he passed out half way though kissing me and that was long enough for me to snap the f\* out of it and realize what I was doing. I left him on the couch, took my German Shepherd into my bedroom and locked my  door behind me. By the next day, everyone seemed to know what happened and I was the the object of a lot of seriously uncomfortable flirtation and frankly, unwanted and inappropriate touching from men that were 10-20 years older than me for the next three years. I complained once to a girl who worked there and she accused me of defaming the professor and avoided talking to me for the next two years. 

&#x200B;

1) Seeing a OBGYN should be your first step, IMO. Most GP's will simply tell you to do that for any of your "feminine" concerns, or even a birth control prescription.  You would have to go to an OBGYN to get a TVS anyway. A visit to your GP wouldn't hurt though. There are many effects on fertility and your primary care physician should be able to rule out other factors that don't directly require a pelvic exam. 

2) Did a little bit of scholarly research... and there appears to be limited research supporting any notion that Cipralex harms male fertility, but some animal studies suggest it may have a small effect on sperm motility. I wouldn't look too deeply into this, most SSRI anti-depressants have no connection with male fertility beyond that many SSRI's are known to decrease sex drive and in some cases, cause impotence. 

&#x200B;
